<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1003–</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Savoy is a dynasty founded in the early eleventh century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert I, Count of Savoy</a>, in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. It became dukes of Savoy in 1416, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, took the royal title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1713 and exchanged it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1720. Its kings led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> and reigned over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1861 until the monarchy was abolished in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy.jpg" alt="House of Savoy" width="320" height="538" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ddurbmonnejg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesser_coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_(1890)_(2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its driving force was the Kingdom of Sardinia, ruled from Turin by the <b>House of Savoy</b> and guided by its prime minister, Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>House of Savoy</b> the island remained distinct from the much richer mainland of Piedmont, with its silk industry, wine and the growing city of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, however, its republican content did not suit the monarchy, which kept the <b>House of Savoy</b>'s &quot;Marcia Reale&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>House of Savoy</b>, now kings of the Kingdom of Sardinia, long sought to absorb the enclave.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-masserano">Principality of Masserano</a></li></ul></section>
